--- a/asm/bitcoin_utxo_lsm.asm
+++ b/asm/bitcoin_utxo_lsm.asm
@@ utxo_lsm_reload_ro's WAL open (the O_RDONLY open near its head) @@
     ; RPC-3 (2026-09-05, /mnt/2tbssd benchmark): the READ-ONLY reload opens
     ; the WAL O_RDONLY already (never utxo.idx -- that is the whole point of
     ; the _ro variant), but the drain loop then takes the writer slot to
     ; rebuild the memtable, exactly like the rw reload. On a live datadir
     ; with the download worker mid-flush that drain cannot finish, and every
     ; _ro caller -- the gettxoutsetinfo RPC path and every offline tool
     ; (utxo_setinfo --muhash, utxo_dump_keys, utxo_probe_one) -- either
     ; waited indefinitely inside the drain or saw the caller-side exit(1).
     ;
     ; The fix is NOT a lock. reload_ro rebuilds the memtable by replaying the
     ; WAL and committed runs; it does not need to DRAIN concurrently with the
     ; writer if it replays against a stable snapshot. Approach: open the WAL
     ; O_RDONLY AND the manifest read-only, replay every run + the WAL tail
     ; with the generation/offsets the manifest carries, and return -1 only on
     ; a genuine format error. The invariant that makes this safe on a live
     ; store: the WAL and run files are append-only while a generation is
     ; live -- the compactor never truncates the WAL until the writers rotate
     ; generations, so a read-only replay of [manifest runs + WAL up to
     ; log_len captured at open] is a consistent snapshot. Callers tolerate
     ; a slightly stale set (a set-info hash can be milliseconds behind; the
     ; node's own applied height is reported from utxo_applied_height.dat).
     ;
     ; NOTE to the implementing session: this is an asm change; reproduce the
     ; snapshot semantics with a REGTEST harness (test_utxo_lsm_reload_ro)
     ; that hammers a writer and a reload_ro concurrently and asserts (a) no
     ; hang, (b) every key present in the snapshot was durably WAL'd before
     ; open. If the snapshot path is too invasive for asm in this round, the
     ; interim step is the caller-side deadline (RPC-1) so a blocked reload_ro
     ; at least returns "rpcservertimeout" instead of hanging an RPC worker.
